### WORKSPACE — every endpoint this feature exposes, plus the failure probes.
###
### REST Client format; runs from the IDE with no external tool. It versions with the code, so it
### never drifts from the routes it probes (QUALITY-01). The smoke test is the guarantee that runs in
### CI; this is the manual probe for when something still surprises you.
###
### Run the requests in order. The session and the cross-site token are both cookies, so the client
### carries them forward on its own once request 0a has been refused — that refusal is what mints the
### token. The token is read back out of the jar with {{$cookie XSRF-TOKEN}}: it is minted per session
### by the server, so it cannot live in an env file and must not be pasted by hand.
###
### The passcode is not in any response by design — it exists only in the delivered message. Read it
### from Mailpit at http://localhost:8025 and paste it into the signup request below.

@host = http://localhost:8081
@owner = maria@atelier.ro
@password = a-long-enough-passphrase

### 0a. Establish the cross-site token. 401 is the expected answer; the cookie is the point.
GET {{host}}/api/auth/session

### 0b. Request the passcode, then read it from Mailpit.
POST {{host}}/api/auth/signup/passcode
Content-Type: application/json

{"email": "{{owner}}"}

### 0c. Become the owner. Paste the passcode from Mailpit. Expect 201 and a SESSION cookie.
POST {{host}}/api/auth/signup
Content-Type: application/json
X-XSRF-TOKEN: {{$cookie XSRF-TOKEN}}

{"email": "{{owner}}", "passcode": "PASTE-FROM-MAILPIT", "password": "{{password}}"}

### 1. The prefill. Expect 200, setupCompleted false, a suggested zone, and the valid set.
GET {{host}}/api/workspace/setup

### 2. Set the workspace up. Expect 200, the workspace, and the landing target.
POST {{host}}/api/workspace/setup
Content-Type: application/json
X-XSRF-TOKEN: {{$cookie XSRF-TOKEN}}

{
  "ownerName": "Maria Ionescu",
  "workspaceName": "Atelier Ionescu",
  "use": "WORK",
  "timezone": "Europe/Bucharest"
}

### 3. Replay it. Expect 200, the workspace unchanged, and no second event — reaching setup twice
### is a routing accident, not an error.
POST {{host}}/api/workspace/setup
Content-Type: application/json
X-XSRF-TOKEN: {{$cookie XSRF-TOKEN}}

{
  "ownerName": "Someone Else",
  "workspaceName": "A Different Name",
  "use": "PERSONAL",
  "timezone": "Europe/London"
}

### --- Failure probes. Each one exists so a live 404 versus 403 versus 500 is one keystroke away. ---

### 4. No session. Expect 401 and no body — the chain refuses before the endpoint runs.
### A 404 here would mean the route is not registered at all, which is the failure this probe is for.
GET {{host}}/api/workspace/setup
Cookie:

### 5. No cross-site token, session present. Expect 403.
POST {{host}}/api/workspace/setup
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "ownerName": "Maria Ionescu",
  "workspaceName": "Atelier Ionescu",
  "use": "WORK",
  "timezone": "Europe/Bucharest"
}

### 6. Bad cross-site token. Expect 403.
POST {{host}}/api/workspace/setup
Content-Type: application/json
X-XSRF-TOKEN: not-the-token-you-were-given

{
  "ownerName": "Maria Ionescu",
  "workspaceName": "Atelier Ionescu",
  "use": "WORK",
  "timezone": "Europe/Bucharest"
}

### 7. Empty workspace name. Expect 400, code REQUEST_INVALID, details naming workspaceName / NotBlank.
### Not WORKSPACE_NAME_REQUIRED: bean validation on the request refuses a blank name before the use
### case runs, so the domain's own refusal is unreachable over HTTP. The field is named either way.
POST {{host}}/api/workspace/setup
Content-Type: application/json
X-XSRF-TOKEN: {{$cookie XSRF-TOKEN}}

{
  "ownerName": "Maria Ionescu",
  "workspaceName": "   ",
  "use": "WORK",
  "timezone": "Europe/Bucharest"
}

### 8. Empty owner name. Expect 400, code REQUEST_INVALID, details naming ownerName / NotBlank.
### Same reason as probe 7.
POST {{host}}/api/workspace/setup
Content-Type: application/json
X-XSRF-TOKEN: {{$cookie XSRF-TOKEN}}

{
  "ownerName": "   ",
  "workspaceName": "Atelier Ionescu",
  "use": "WORK",
  "timezone": "Europe/Bucharest"
}

### 9. A zone the runtime does not know. Expect 400, code TIMEZONE_UNKNOWN.
### This is the field that produces no error anywhere else and quietly skews every later measurement.
POST {{host}}/api/workspace/setup
Content-Type: application/json
X-XSRF-TOKEN: {{$cookie XSRF-TOKEN}}

{
  "ownerName": "Maria Ionescu",
  "workspaceName": "Atelier Ionescu",
  "use": "WORK",
  "timezone": "Europe/Atlantis"
}

### 10. A fixed offset rather than a zone. Expect 400 — it parses, and it is wrong twice a year.
POST {{host}}/api/workspace/setup
Content-Type: application/json
X-XSRF-TOKEN: {{$cookie XSRF-TOKEN}}

{
  "ownerName": "Maria Ionescu",
  "workspaceName": "Atelier Ionescu",
  "use": "WORK",
  "timezone": "+02:00"
}

### 11. Malformed body. Expect 400 and the shared envelope, never a stack trace.
POST {{host}}/api/workspace/setup
Content-Type: application/json
X-XSRF-TOKEN: {{$cookie XSRF-TOKEN}}

{"ownerName": "Maria Ionescu"}

### 12. Someone who does not own setup. Sign in as a colleague first, then expect 403 and
### code SETUP_NOT_PERMITTED — on the prefill as well as on the action. There is no such colleague
### until WORKSPACE-INVITE-01 is built.
POST {{host}}/api/workspace/setup
Content-Type: application/json
X-XSRF-TOKEN: {{$cookie XSRF-TOKEN}}

{
  "ownerName": "Ionuț Ștefănescu",
  "workspaceName": "Altă Firmă",
  "use": "WORK",
  "timezone": "Europe/Bucharest"
}

###############################################################################
# WORKSPACE-INVITE-01 — invite a person with a role and a reporting line
#
# Requires PERSON_INVITE, which in this pass the owner alone holds. Manager
# invitations and the owner-approval switch are specified and deferred together
# (DECISION-MVP-TRIAGE-01), so there is no probe for AWAITING_APPROVAL — it has
# no producer yet, and a probe for a state nothing creates would read as a gap
# rather than as a deferral.
#
# No probe here carries a token, and none can: the token is never returned by
# any endpoint. Replace {{managerId}} with the owner's own membership id, which
# is the only active membership until the first invitation is accepted.
###############################################################################

### 13. Invite a colleague. Expect 201, state SENT, an expiry seven days out,
### and NO token anywhere in the response body.
POST {{host}}/api/workspace/invitations
Content-Type: application/json
X-XSRF-TOKEN: {{$cookie XSRF-TOKEN}}

{
  "emailAddress": "ionut@atelier.ro",
  "role": "EMPLOYEE",
  "managerId": "{{managerId}}"
}

### 14. The same address again while the first invitation is open.
### Expect 409 and code DUPLICATE_INVITATION — not a second message to Ionuț.
POST {{host}}/api/workspace/invitations
Content-Type: application/json
X-XSRF-TOKEN: {{$cookie XSRF-TOKEN}}

{
  "emailAddress": "IONUT@Atelier.ro",
  "role": "EMPLOYEE",
  "managerId": "{{managerId}}"
}

### 15. The caller's own address. Expect 422 and code SELF_INVITATION —
### distinct from ALREADY_MEMBER, because "you are already here" and "somebody
### else holds that" send a person to different places.
POST {{host}}/api/workspace/invitations
Content-Type: application/json
X-XSRF-TOKEN: {{$cookie XSRF-TOKEN}}

{
  "emailAddress": "maria@atelier.ro",
  "role": "EMPLOYEE",
  "managerId": "{{managerId}}"
}

### 16. A manager identifier that is not an active membership.
### Expect 422 and code MANAGER_INACTIVE — checked at submission, not at render.
POST {{host}}/api/workspace/invitations
Content-Type: application/json
X-XSRF-TOKEN: {{$cookie XSRF-TOKEN}}

{
  "emailAddress": "ioana@atelier.ro",
  "role": "EMPLOYEE",
  "managerId": "00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000"
}

### 17. The owner role, which the request type cannot express.
### Expect 400 from deserialisation: nobody may invite an owner, including the
### owner, so the enum has no such constant to submit.
POST {{host}}/api/workspace/invitations
Content-Type: application/json
X-XSRF-TOKEN: {{$cookie XSRF-TOKEN}}

{
  "emailAddress": "cineva@atelier.ro",
  "role": "OWNER",
  "managerId": "{{managerId}}"
}

### 18. Malformed address. Expect 400 and the shared envelope naming the field.
POST {{host}}/api/workspace/invitations
Content-Type: application/json
X-XSRF-TOKEN: {{$cookie XSRF-TOKEN}}

{
  "emailAddress": "not-an-address",
  "role": "EMPLOYEE",
  "managerId": "{{managerId}}"
}

### 19. No session. Expect 401 from the security chain, with no body.
POST {{host}}/api/workspace/invitations
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "emailAddress": "ionut@atelier.ro",
  "role": "EMPLOYEE",
  "managerId": "{{managerId}}"
}

### 20. Session but no cross-site token. Expect 403 from the CSRF filter,
### before the endpoint is reached at all.
POST {{host}}/api/workspace/invitations
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "emailAddress": "ionut@atelier.ro",
  "role": "EMPLOYEE",
  "managerId": "{{managerId}}"
}

### 21. The fourth invitation to one address inside a day, after three have been
### created and declined or revoked. Expect 429 and code RATE_LIMIT, and no
### message sent — the bound that makes this endpoint safe to publish.
POST {{host}}/api/workspace/invitations
Content-Type: application/json
X-XSRF-TOKEN: {{$cookie XSRF-TOKEN}}

{
  "emailAddress": "ionut@atelier.ro",
  "role": "EMPLOYEE",
  "managerId": "{{managerId}}"
}

### --- WORKSPACE-VIEW-PEOPLE-01 — the directory and the reporting line ---

### 22. The people screen, as the owner. Expect 200: every membership with its
### display name, role and managerId, plus `invitations` because the owner holds
### PERSON_INVITE, plus `onlyMember`.
###
### Read the body for two absences rather than for what is present. No member
### row carries an address — only display names — and nothing anywhere counts,
### scores, averages or ranks anybody's work (CONSTRAINT-METRICS-PRIVACY-01).
### The owner's row has managerId null, which is invariant I2: the tree is
### rooted at the owner and the root is the membership with no manager.
GET {{host}}/api/workspace/people

### 23. The same request as somebody who does not hold PERSON_INVITE — sign in
### as an employee first. Expect 200, and expect the `invitations` field to be
### **absent from the JSON entirely**, not present and empty.
###
### The difference is the point. An empty array is the claim "there are none",
### which is a different and possibly false thing to tell somebody; an
### unannounced hire is information about the business and does not reach people
### with no part in the decision. If you see `"invitations": []` here, the
### server-side filter has been replaced by a client-side one and the disclosure
### has already happened.
GET {{host}}/api/workspace/people

### 24. No session. Expect 401 from the security chain, with no body — the same
### answer an address that does not exist would give, so nothing is learned
### about what the product has.
GET {{host}}/api/workspace/people

### 25. Authenticated but without PEOPLE_VIEW. Expect 403.
###
### Unreachable as shipped: V9 grants PEOPLE_VIEW to owner, manager and employee
### alike, so no real caller can be refused. To see it, delete the row —
###   delete from auth_role_permission
###    where role_name = 'EMPLOYEE' and permission_name = 'PEOPLE_VIEW';
### — sign in as an employee, run this, and put the row back. That is exactly
### what the round-trip test does, and it is the only way to show the annotation
### is load-bearing rather than decorative.
GET {{host}}/api/workspace/people

### --- WORKSPACE-REVOKE-INVITE-01 — withdraw an invitation before it is accepted ---

### 26. Withdraw the invitation created by probe 17. Expect 200, state REVOKED,
### and a revokedAt. Run probe 22 afterwards: the invitation is gone from the
### directory, which is where the person acted from.
###
### The response carries no token and no hash — there is no field for either.
POST {{host}}/api/workspace/invitations/{{invitationId}}/revoke
Content-Type: application/json
X-XSRF-TOKEN: {{$cookie XSRF-TOKEN}}

### 27. The same call again. Expect 200, state still REVOKED, and **revokedAt
### null** — this call changed nothing, so it withdrew nothing.
###
### Check `select count(*) from workspace_event where action = 'INVITATION_REVOKED'`
### after: it must still be 1. Two events would have the record claim an
### invitation was withdrawn twice, which is the failure the row lock prevents.
POST {{host}}/api/workspace/invitations/{{invitationId}}/revoke
Content-Type: application/json
X-XSRF-TOKEN: {{$cookie XSRF-TOKEN}}

### 28. An identifier naming no invitation. Expect 404 and INVITATION_NOT_FOUND.
###
### A refusal rather than a silent success, unlike the already-terminal case
### above: "this invitation cannot be accepted" is true of one that never
### existed, and answering success would also be true of a typo, leaving the
### caller believing they had withdrawn something.
POST {{host}}/api/workspace/invitations/00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000/revoke
Content-Type: application/json
X-XSRF-TOKEN: {{$cookie XSRF-TOKEN}}

### 29. No session. Expect 401 from the security chain, with no body.
POST {{host}}/api/workspace/invitations/{{invitationId}}/revoke
Content-Type: application/json

### 30. Holding neither revoke permission. Expect 403.
###
### Unreachable as shipped — V10 grants the owner both. To see it:
###   delete from auth_role_permission where role_name = 'OWNER'
###    and permission_name in ('INVITATION_REVOKE_OWN', 'INVITATION_REVOKE_ANY');
### then sign in, run this, and put the rows back. Both go, not one: the
### annotation accepts either, which is decision row 91.
POST {{host}}/api/workspace/invitations/{{invitationId}}/revoke
Content-Type: application/json
X-XSRF-TOKEN: {{$cookie XSRF-TOKEN}}

### 31. NOT_YOURS — a manager reaching for an invitation somebody else created.
### Expect 403 and code NOT_YOURS, told apart from probe 30 by the code rather
### than the status, because both answer 403.
###
### Unreachable until AUTH-ACCEPT-INVITE-01 ships: there is no second person to
### hold the manager role. The rule is built and unit-tested against a manager
### who does hold it.
POST {{host}}/api/workspace/invitations/{{invitationId}}/revoke
Content-Type: application/json
X-XSRF-TOKEN: {{$cookie XSRF-TOKEN}}

### 32. ALREADY_ACCEPTED — expect 409, and a message naming the person and
### naming deactivation without offering it, because
### WORKSPACE-DEACTIVATE-PERSON-01 is Specced and not built.
###
### Also unreachable until acceptance ships: nothing sets ACCEPTED.
POST {{host}}/api/workspace/invitations/{{invitationId}}/revoke
Content-Type: application/json
X-XSRF-TOKEN: {{$cookie XSRF-TOKEN}}

### --- WORKSPACE-EDIT-REPORTING-LINE-01 — change who a person reports to ---

### 33. What the move would do, before doing it. Expect 200 with the former and
### new manager by name, and `movingWithThem` naming everybody who travels with
### the person because they report to them.
###
### Read the list, not the count: extension 3a exists because moving one person
### can silently relocate a third of the company. There is no separate count
### field — it is the list's length, so the two cannot disagree.
GET {{host}}/api/workspace/people/{{personId}}/manager/preview?proposedManagerId={{managerId}}

### 34. Make the move. Expect 200, `changed: true`, and exactly one
### REPORTING_LINE_CHANGED row in workspace_event.
###
### The event is not optional and not decoration. Moving a person under yourself
### grants visibility over their work with no permission having changed, so
### `select * from workspace_event where action = 'REPORTING_LINE_CHANGED'` is
### the only place the question "how did this manager come to see this?" can be
### answered from.
POST {{host}}/api/workspace/people/{{personId}}/manager
Content-Type: application/json
X-XSRF-TOKEN: {{$cookie XSRF-TOKEN}}

{
  "proposedManagerId": "{{managerId}}"
}

### 35. The same call again. Expect 200 and `changed: false` — the proposal is
### already true. Nothing is written and **no second event is appended**, because
### the log records what happened and nothing did.
POST {{host}}/api/workspace/people/{{personId}}/manager
Content-Type: application/json
X-XSRF-TOKEN: {{$cookie XSRF-TOKEN}}

{
  "proposedManagerId": "{{managerId}}"
}

### 36. CYCLE — name somebody below this person as their manager. Expect 422,
### code CYCLE, and `details` carrying the path that closes the loop, one step
### per entry, from the person being moved down to the proposed manager.
###
### This is the only refusal in the feature a reasonable person triggers by
### accident, which is why it carries the path instead of only saying no. The
### screen turns it into "Andrei reports to Ioana, who reports to Ionuț. So Ionuț
### cannot report to Andrei."
POST {{host}}/api/workspace/people/{{managerId}}/manager
Content-Type: application/json
X-XSRF-TOKEN: {{$cookie XSRF-TOKEN}}

{
  "proposedManagerId": "{{personId}}"
}

### 37. SELF_MANAGER. Expect 422 — a cycle of length one is still a cycle.
POST {{host}}/api/workspace/people/{{personId}}/manager
Content-Type: application/json
X-XSRF-TOKEN: {{$cookie XSRF-TOKEN}}

{
  "proposedManagerId": "{{personId}}"
}

### 38. OWNER_HAS_NO_MANAGER — the owner is the root (invariant I2). Expect 422.
### Checked before anything about the proposal, because it is a fact about the
### subject: telling somebody their chosen manager is ineligible when the real
### answer is that this person cannot have one at all sends them to change the
### wrong field.
POST {{host}}/api/workspace/people/{{ownerMembershipId}}/manager
Content-Type: application/json
X-XSRF-TOKEN: {{$cookie XSRF-TOKEN}}

{
  "proposedManagerId": "{{managerId}}"
}

### 39. MANAGER_NOT_ELIGIBLE — name somebody holding the employee role. Expect
### 422. Invariant I3's second half: a person with reports gains visibility over
### other people's work, and that visibility is what the manager role *is*.
POST {{host}}/api/workspace/people/{{personId}}/manager
Content-Type: application/json
X-XSRF-TOKEN: {{$cookie XSRF-TOKEN}}

{
  "proposedManagerId": "{{employeeMembershipId}}"
}

### 40. MANAGER_INACTIVE — name somebody deactivated. Expect 422, told apart from
### probe 39 by the code rather than the status, because both answer 422 and they
### send the person somewhere different: pick somebody still here, or pick
### somebody who may hold reports.
POST {{host}}/api/workspace/people/{{personId}}/manager
Content-Type: application/json
X-XSRF-TOKEN: {{$cookie XSRF-TOKEN}}

{
  "proposedManagerId": "{{deactivatedMembershipId}}"
}

### 41. SUBJECT_INACTIVE — move somebody who was deactivated. Expect 422, and the
### code rather than the status is what separates it from probe 40: that one is
### about the person being moved *to*, this one about the person being moved. The
### directory hides the row action on a deactivated row, so this probe is the only
### way to reach the rule — which is the reason it is a rule and not a hidden
### control. The precondition has always said it; until now only the owner half of
### it was enforced.
POST {{host}}/api/workspace/people/{{deactivatedMembershipId}}/manager
Content-Type: application/json
X-XSRF-TOKEN: {{$cookie XSRF-TOKEN}}

{
  "proposedManagerId": "{{managerId}}"
}

### 42. MEMBERSHIP_NOT_FOUND. Expect 404 — answered for the subject and the
### proposed manager alike, because distinguishing them would tell a caller which
### of two identifiers exists.
POST {{host}}/api/workspace/people/00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000/manager
Content-Type: application/json
X-XSRF-TOKEN: {{$cookie XSRF-TOKEN}}

{
  "proposedManagerId": "{{managerId}}"
}

### 43. Without REPORTING_LINE_EDIT. Expect 403 and NOT_PERMITTED.
###
### Unreachable as shipped: V11 grants it to the owner, and to nobody else on
### purpose. To see it:
###   delete from auth_role_permission
###    where role_name = 'OWNER' and permission_name = 'REPORTING_LINE_EDIT';
### then sign in, run this, and put the row back. Widening this permission is not
### a configuration change — WORKSPACE_UC_04 warns the containment check it would
### need fails silently.
POST {{host}}/api/workspace/people/{{personId}}/manager
Content-Type: application/json
X-XSRF-TOKEN: {{$cookie XSRF-TOKEN}}

{
  "proposedManagerId": "{{managerId}}"
}

### AUTH-ACCEPT-INVITE-01 — see what an invitation offers, with no session at all.
###
### The only endpoint in WORKSPACE reachable without signing in. To try it: run request 8 above to
### create an invitation, take the token out of the link in the message the server sent, and paste it
### here. The token is stored nowhere — the mail is the only place it ever exists.
###
### Send this one from a fresh client, or with the session cookie removed. If it answers while you
### are signed in but 401 when you are not, the public path entry is missing and the screen an
### invited person opens will never work.
GET {{host}}/api/workspace/invitations/{{invitationToken}}
Accept-Language: ro

### 24. The same address with a token that was never minted — 410 INVITATION_NOT_USABLE.
### Expired, already used, revoked and awaiting-approval all answer exactly this, byte for byte.
### If any of them differs, criterion 5 is broken and the difference tells whoever holds the token
### that an address is under consideration.
GET {{host}}/api/workspace/invitations/a-token-that-was-never-minted
Accept-Language: en

### 25. AUTH-ACCEPT-INVITE-01 slice B — agree and join, with no session at all.
###
### Run request 23 first. That GET is not optional here: it is what mints the XSRF-TOKEN cookie, and
### unlike the read this is a POST, so the cross-site token applies. `consentVersion` must be the
### version request 23 answered with — send a different one and it answers 409 CONSENT_VERSION_STALE,
### which is the point: nobody is recorded as agreeing to words they did not read.
###
### Expect 201, a SESSION cookie, and a landing target. Then call GET {{host}}/api/auth/session with
### the same client: it should answer 200 as the person who just joined.
POST {{host}}/api/workspace/invitations/{{invitationToken}}/accept
Content-Type: application/json
X-XSRF-TOKEN: {{$cookie XSRF-TOKEN}}

{"displayName": "Cosmin Ionescu", "password": "{{password}}", "consentVersion": "PASTE-FROM-REQUEST-23"}

### 26. A password that fails policy — 400 PASSWORD_POLICY_VIOLATION, naming the rule.
### `details[0].rule` is the enum constant the frontend turns into a message key. Nothing is created.
POST {{host}}/api/workspace/invitations/{{invitationToken}}/accept
Content-Type: application/json
X-XSRF-TOKEN: {{$cookie XSRF-TOKEN}}

{"displayName": "Cosmin Ionescu", "password": "short", "consentVersion": "PASTE-FROM-REQUEST-23"}

### 27. An empty name — 400 DISPLAY_NAME_REQUIRED, and nothing is created.
### The invitation carries an address and never a name, so this is the only moment to ask for one.
POST {{host}}/api/workspace/invitations/{{invitationToken}}/accept
Content-Type: application/json
X-XSRF-TOKEN: {{$cookie XSRF-TOKEN}}

{"displayName": "   ", "password": "{{password}}", "consentVersion": "PASTE-FROM-REQUEST-23"}

### 28. Decline — 204, and nothing is created.
### Afterwards the invitation reads DECLINED with a declined_at, there is no account and no
### membership, and the event log holds INVITATION_DECLINED and nothing claiming anybody joined.
POST {{host}}/api/workspace/invitations/{{invitationToken}}/decline
X-XSRF-TOKEN: {{$cookie XSRF-TOKEN}}

### 29. The carve-out is one path segment wide, and this is how you check it.
### Accepting and declining are public; revoking sits at the same depth and must stay 401 to a caller
### with no session. Send this one with the session cookie removed — a 200 here is a stranger being
### able to withdraw somebody's invitation.
POST {{host}}/api/workspace/invitations/{{invitationId}}/revoke
X-XSRF-TOKEN: {{$cookie XSRF-TOKEN}}


### ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
### WORKSPACE-ERASE-PERSON-01 -- the only irreversible operation in the product.
### Three barriers stand in front of it and each removes a different failure, so each has its own
### probe below: prior deactivation removes the accidental single click, re-authentication removes the
### unattended session, and the typed name removes the wrong row.
### ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

### 30. What erasing somebody would destroy -- 200, and nothing is written.
### `destroys` and `survives` are keys the interface translates, never sentences. `refusal` names the
### barrier in the way rather than throwing, so the screen can explain it where the owner is looking.
### Note what is NOT in this response: no email address. The directory forbids that shape and so does
### this -- a response carrying something the interface then hides has already disclosed it.
GET {{host}}/api/workspace/people/{{membershipId}}/erasure

### 31. Erase, with no re-authentication -- 403 REAUTHENTICATION_REQUIRED, and nothing is destroyed.
### Run this BEFORE probe 32. It is the barrier that would have answered 500: AUTH's guard throws
### AUTH's exception and AUTH's advice is scoped to AUTH's package, so WORKSPACE needed its own.
POST {{host}}/api/workspace/people/{{membershipId}}/erase
Content-Type: application/json
X-XSRF-TOKEN: {{$cookie XSRF-TOKEN}}

{"typedName": "Ionut Petrescu"}

### 32. Elevate the session, which is the second barrier satisfied.
### The password is spent here and never travels with the destructive request itself.
POST {{host}}/api/auth/reauthenticate
Content-Type: application/json
X-XSRF-TOKEN: {{$cookie XSRF-TOKEN}}

{"password": "{{password}}"}

### 33. Erase somebody still working here -- 422 SUBJECT_ACTIVE.
### The first barrier. Point this at an ACTIVE membership; erasing is two deliberate acts and this is
### the refusal that makes it two.
POST {{host}}/api/workspace/people/{{activeMembershipId}}/erase
Content-Type: application/json
X-XSRF-TOKEN: {{$cookie XSRF-TOKEN}}

{"typedName": "Ioana Radu"}

### 34. Erase with a colleague's name typed instead -- 422 NAME_MISMATCH, and nothing is destroyed.
### The third barrier. Use a name that belongs to somebody real: against a nonsense string a guard that
### refuses everything and one that compares correctly are indistinguishable, and the mistake this
### exists to catch is the right person acting deliberately on the wrong row.
POST {{host}}/api/workspace/people/{{membershipId}}/erase
Content-Type: application/json
X-XSRF-TOKEN: {{$cookie XSRF-TOKEN}}

{"typedName": "Ioana Radu"}

### 35. Erase -- 200, and it cannot be undone.
### Afterwards: the membership reads ERASED with an erased_at and its deactivated_at intact, the
### account holds an opaque erased+<id>@erased.invalid address and no display name, auth_credential has
### no row for it, and the directory returns the person under no filter at all.
### `opaqueIdentifier` is the account identifier -- everything they authored already resolves through
### it, so nothing is minted and nothing is rewritten.
POST {{host}}/api/workspace/people/{{membershipId}}/erase
Content-Type: application/json
X-XSRF-TOKEN: {{$cookie XSRF-TOKEN}}

{"typedName": "Ionut Petrescu"}

### 36. Erase the same person again -- 200 with `changed: false`, and no second event.
### There is nothing left to destroy, so the caller's intent is already true. Check the event table
### afterwards: exactly one PERSON_ERASED, not two.
POST {{host}}/api/workspace/people/{{membershipId}}/erase
Content-Type: application/json
X-XSRF-TOKEN: {{$cookie XSRF-TOKEN}}

{"typedName": "anything at all"}

### 37. The refusal for a caller who holds no PERSON_ERASE -- 403 NOT_PERMITTED.
### Sign in as an employee first. Owner-only and never delegable: erasure is the one operation where a
### mistaken grant of authority cannot be corrected afterwards.
POST {{host}}/api/workspace/people/{{membershipId}}/erase
Content-Type: application/json
X-XSRF-TOKEN: {{$cookie XSRF-TOKEN}}

{"typedName": "Ionut Petrescu"}


### ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
### WORKSPACE-VIEW-OWN-DATA-01 and WORKSPACE-EDIT-OWN-PROFILE-01 -- the right of access, and my name.
### Note what is missing from every probe below: a parameter naming whose data to return. That absence
### is the access control, not an oversight -- an endpoint accepting one would be a decision waiting to
### be got wrong. The single exception is probe 42, and it is bounded twice.
### ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

### 38. Everything held about me -- 200.
### Read the response for what is NOT in it: no colleague's address, no task content, no comment text.
### The only other person named anywhere is a manager in the caller's own reporting line, and that line
### is the caller's data -- it is the record of who could see their work.
GET {{host}}/api/workspace/me

### 39. Take a copy -- 200, delivered as an attachment.
### Same use case and same mapper as probe 38, because criterion 3 says the file carries the same
### information as the screen and two readers would drift. This is the one read on this path that
### writes: a row in workspace_data_export naming the subject, the producer and the moment.
POST {{host}}/api/workspace/me/export
X-XSRF-TOKEN: {{$cookie XSRF-TOKEN}}

### 40. The sixth copy in a day -- 429 EXPORT_LIMIT.
### Send probe 39 five times first. Asserting that the first succeeds proves nothing about a bound; the
### sixth is the one that has to be told no. An export reads across every table holding anything about
### somebody, so an unbounded loop of them is aimed at the person's own workspace.
POST {{host}}/api/workspace/me/export
X-XSRF-TOKEN: {{$cookie XSRF-TOKEN}}

### 41. The owner produces a deactivated person's copy -- 200, and the file says who made it.
### Extension 3a: the right of access does not lapse when access does. Deactivate somebody first.
### `producedForSomebodyElse` is true, and `account.sessions` is empty because deactivation ended them.
POST {{host}}/api/workspace/people/{{deactivatedMembershipId}}/export
X-XSRF-TOKEN: {{$cookie XSRF-TOKEN}}

### 42. The same, aimed at somebody still working here -- 422 SUBJECT_ACTIVE.
### They can sign in and ask for their own copy, which is the path this one substitutes for. Check
### afterwards that workspace_data_export has no row: a refusal must not spend one of their five.
POST {{host}}/api/workspace/people/{{activeMembershipId}}/export
X-XSRF-TOKEN: {{$cookie XSRF-TOKEN}}

### 43. The same, as an employee -- 403 NOT_PERMITTED.
### Sign in as an employee. PERSON_DEACTIVATE is owner-only and non-delegable, and its population is
### exactly the people this path may reach -- which is why it is the permission borrowed here rather
### than a new one invented in a specification that is not mine.
POST {{host}}/api/workspace/people/{{deactivatedMembershipId}}/export
X-XSRF-TOKEN: {{$cookie XSRF-TOKEN}}

### 44. Change my own name -- 200 with `changed: true`.
### Afterwards the directory reads the new name everywhere, on things authored before the change too,
### because nothing stores a copy of it. The event holds the person, the actor and the moment and
### NEITHER name -- owner ruling on WORKSPACE_REQ_BLOCKER_06, option A. Check workspace_event: no row
### contains either spelling.
PUT {{host}}/api/workspace/me/profile
Content-Type: application/json
X-XSRF-TOKEN: {{$cookie XSRF-TOKEN}}

{"displayName": "Ioana Radu-Marin"}

### 45. Submit the same name again -- 200 with `changed: false`, and no second event.
### Extension 1b. Count PROFILE_CHANGED afterwards: one, not two.
PUT {{host}}/api/workspace/me/profile
Content-Type: application/json
X-XSRF-TOKEN: {{$cookie XSRF-TOKEN}}

{"displayName": "Ioana Radu-Marin"}

### 46. A name of nothing but spaces -- 400, and nothing is stored.
### Bean validation refuses this before the use case runs, so over HTTP this proves the boundary and
### not the use case's own guard. That guard is real and is proven where it is reachable, in
### EditOwnProfileServiceTest -- two walls, and only one of them is visible from here.
PUT {{host}}/api/workspace/me/profile
Content-Type: application/json
X-XSRF-TOKEN: {{$cookie XSRF-TOKEN}}

{"displayName": "   "}

### 47. An attempt to change the address -- there is no field for it, and adding one changes nothing.
### Extension 1c. The address is what the invitation, the consent record and the credential are bound
### to. The extra property is ignored and the name is what changes.
PUT {{host}}/api/workspace/me/profile
Content-Type: application/json
X-XSRF-TOKEN: {{$cookie XSRF-TOKEN}}

{"displayName": "Ioana Radu-Marin", "emailAddress": "somebody-else@atelier.ro"}


### ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
### WORKSPACE-CONFIGURE-01 -- quietly the most consequential screen in the product. Every number TRIAGE
### shows and every nudge AUTOMATION sends is computed against these values, and nothing validates them
### against reality: a wrong working day produces no error, only confidently incorrect metrics.
### Owner-only. Sign in as the owner before any of these.
### ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

### 48. How the business is measured -- 200, and never empty.
### Shipping defaults apply until somebody changes them: Monday to Friday, nine to five, a 24-hour
### at-risk window, escalation at 24/72/168 hours, quiet hours 22:00 to 06:00, approval switch off.
### `effectiveFrom` is here because a person asking why a number looks wrong is asking exactly that.
GET {{host}}/api/workspace/settings

### 49. Change two values -- 200, and `changedFields` names exactly those two.
### Afterwards there are TWO rows in workspace_settings: the old one closed at the instant the new one
### opened. That is what lets a measurement taken last month resolve last month's parameters. And
### workspace_settings_change holds one row per field that moved, with its old and new value -- the
### event alone would say something changed and be unable to say what.
PUT {{host}}/api/workspace/settings
Content-Type: application/json
X-XSRF-TOKEN: {{$cookie XSRF-TOKEN}}

{"name": "Atelier Bucuresti", "use": "WORK", "timezone": "Europe/Bucharest",
 "workingDays": ["MONDAY","TUESDAY","WEDNESDAY","THURSDAY","FRIDAY"],
 "workingHoursStart": "09:00", "workingHoursEnd": "17:00",
 "atRiskWindowHours": 48,
 "escalationIntervalsHours": [24, 72, 168],
 "quietHoursStart": "22:00", "quietHoursEnd": "06:00",
 "invitationApprovalRequired": true}

### 50. Send exactly the same values again -- 200, and `changedFields` is EMPTY.
### Extension 5b. Check afterwards: no third settings row, and still one SETTINGS_CHANGED event rather
### than two. The record is a history of changes, not a history of visits -- and superseding a row with
### an identical one would end its period for no reason, which is the one way to corrupt the history.
PUT {{host}}/api/workspace/settings
Content-Type: application/json
X-XSRF-TOKEN: {{$cookie XSRF-TOKEN}}

{"name": "Atelier Bucuresti", "use": "WORK", "timezone": "Europe/Bucharest",
 "workingDays": ["MONDAY","TUESDAY","WEDNESDAY","THURSDAY","FRIDAY"],
 "workingHoursStart": "09:00", "workingHoursEnd": "17:00",
 "atRiskWindowHours": 48,
 "escalationIntervalsHours": [24, 72, 168],
 "quietHoursStart": "22:00", "quietHoursEnd": "06:00",
 "invitationApprovalRequired": true}

### 51. Quiet hours that wrap midnight -- 200. This is the NORMAL case, not an inverted range.
### Extension 3c. Treating it as inverted would either fire escalations through the night or never fire
### them, and both look like a working system from outside.
PUT {{host}}/api/workspace/settings
Content-Type: application/json
X-XSRF-TOKEN: {{$cookie XSRF-TOKEN}}

{"name": "Atelier Bucuresti", "use": "WORK", "timezone": "Europe/Bucharest",
 "workingDays": ["MONDAY","TUESDAY","WEDNESDAY","THURSDAY","FRIDAY"],
 "workingHoursStart": "09:00", "workingHoursEnd": "17:00", "atRiskWindowHours": 48,
 "escalationIntervalsHours": [24, 72, 168],
 "quietHoursStart": "21:30", "quietHoursEnd": "07:15",
 "invitationApprovalRequired": true}

### 52. An escalation ladder that does not ascend -- 422 ESCALATION_INTERVALS_UNORDERED.
### Extension 3a, and `details` names TWO positions rather than one field: an interval is only wrong
### relative to its neighbour, so a refusal naming a single field cannot describe what broke.
PUT {{host}}/api/workspace/settings
Content-Type: application/json
X-XSRF-TOKEN: {{$cookie XSRF-TOKEN}}

{"name": "Atelier Bucuresti", "use": "WORK", "timezone": "Europe/Bucharest",
 "workingDays": ["MONDAY","TUESDAY","WEDNESDAY","THURSDAY","FRIDAY"],
 "workingHoursStart": "09:00", "workingHoursEnd": "17:00", "atRiskWindowHours": 48,
 "escalationIntervalsHours": [24, 72, 48],
 "quietHoursStart": "22:00", "quietHoursEnd": "06:00",
 "invitationApprovalRequired": false}

### 53. Quiet hours covering the whole day -- 422 QUIET_HOURS_COVER_THE_DAY.
### Extension 3d. Escalation would never fire and the product would appear to work while doing nothing,
### which is the failure mode hardest to notice.
PUT {{host}}/api/workspace/settings
Content-Type: application/json
X-XSRF-TOKEN: {{$cookie XSRF-TOKEN}}

{"name": "Atelier Bucuresti", "use": "WORK", "timezone": "Europe/Bucharest",
 "workingDays": ["MONDAY","TUESDAY","WEDNESDAY","THURSDAY","FRIDAY"],
 "workingHoursStart": "09:00", "workingHoursEnd": "17:00", "atRiskWindowHours": 48,
 "escalationIntervalsHours": [24, 72, 168],
 "quietHoursStart": "00:00", "quietHoursEnd": "00:00",
 "invitationApprovalRequired": false}

### 54. No working day -- 422 NO_WORKING_DAYS. Extension 3e: nothing would ever be measured.
PUT {{host}}/api/workspace/settings
Content-Type: application/json
X-XSRF-TOKEN: {{$cookie XSRF-TOKEN}}

{"name": "Atelier Bucuresti", "use": "WORK", "timezone": "Europe/Bucharest",
 "workingDays": [],
 "workingHoursStart": "09:00", "workingHoursEnd": "17:00", "atRiskWindowHours": 48,
 "escalationIntervalsHours": [24, 72, 168],
 "quietHoursStart": "22:00", "quietHoursEnd": "06:00",
 "invitationApprovalRequired": false}

### 55. An at-risk window of zero -- 422 AT_RISK_WINDOW_INVALID.
### Extension 3b: zero marks everything at risk, which is the same as marking nothing. Four refusals
### share the 422, and only the code tells them apart -- which is why each has its own.
PUT {{host}}/api/workspace/settings
Content-Type: application/json
X-XSRF-TOKEN: {{$cookie XSRF-TOKEN}}

{"name": "Atelier Bucuresti", "use": "WORK", "timezone": "Europe/Bucharest",
 "workingDays": ["MONDAY","TUESDAY","WEDNESDAY","THURSDAY","FRIDAY"],
 "workingHoursStart": "09:00", "workingHoursEnd": "17:00", "atRiskWindowHours": 0,
 "escalationIntervalsHours": [24, 72, 168],
 "quietHoursStart": "22:00", "quietHoursEnd": "06:00",
 "invitationApprovalRequired": false}

### 56. Settings as an employee -- 403 NOT_PERMITTED, on both the read and the write.
### Criterion 11. Sign in as an employee first. WORKSPACE_CONFIGURE is owner-only and non-delegable:
### the approval switch decides whether managers may reach outsiders without the owner's consent, so
### turning it off is itself a grant of authority.
GET {{host}}/api/workspace/settings
